-- ============================================================================
-- H1 PROFESSIONAL DEEP SEED: Safety Performance Statistics & KPIs,
-- intermediate tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed, and the question rows from the
-- committed bank JSONs under tools/course-banks/safetystats/intermediate.
--
-- What this tier owns: HOW SURE THE RATE IS. A count is a draw from a Poisson
-- count model; the Garwood exact interval from the chi-square distribution,
-- conservative by construction; confidence as a required fraction and the
-- upper tail solved directly; zero events and the upper limit a zero count
-- still carries; comparing two rates by conditioning on the total; the central
-- p-value the engine reports beside the minlike convention of R and scipy, and
-- why the engine chose central; and intervals set beside published rates.
-- Digest sections 13 to 20, and section 28.
--
-- THE TRAP THE TIER IS BUILT ON. The interval is exact and asymmetric, the
-- zero-event limit is the Garwood limit and sits above the rule of three, and
-- the p-value convention is the one the engine's own rate-ratio interval
-- agrees with. The Professional
-- capstone grades two interval limits, a zero-event limit, a rate-ratio
-- interval and a p-value, and grades no chart.
-- ============================================================================

values ('safetystats', 'intermediate', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-a-count-is-a-draw","title":"A Count Is a Draw","lesson_keys":["l01-the-poisson-count-model","l02-what-an-observed-rate-estimates","l03-small-exposure-and-wide-swings","l04-a-rate-with-no-interval"]},{"key":"m02-the-exact-interval","title":"The Garwood Exact Interval","lesson_keys":["l01-limits-from-the-chi-square-distribution","l02-half-the-miss-in-each-tail","l03-conservative-by-construction","l04-confidence-is-a-required-fraction","l05-the-upper-tail-solved-directly"]},{"key":"m03-zero-events","title":"Zero Events","lesson_keys":["l01-zero-events-is-a-measurement","l02-the-upper-limit-at-zero","l03-the-rule-of-three-and-where-it-differs","l04-how-much-exposure-it-takes"]},{"key":"m04-comparing-two-rates","title":"Comparing Two Rates","lesson_keys":["l01-conditioning-on-the-total-count","l02-the-expected-proportion-from-the-hours","l03-the-rate-ratio-and-its-interval","l04-when-one-group-has-no-events","l05-both-groups-empty-is-refused"]},{"key":"m05-the-p-value-and-its-convention","title":"The p-Value and Its Convention","lesson_keys":["l01-the-central-two-sided-p-value","l02-the-minlike-convention","l03-the-p-value-and-the-interval-agree","l04-no-difference-found-is-a-weak-claim"]},{"key":"m06-intervals-in-a-report","title":"Intervals in a Report","lesson_keys":["l01-the-bls-case-with-its-interval","l02-the-iogp-far-with-its-interval","l03-writing-a-rate-with-its-uncertainty","l04-the-capstone-brief"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
